{
  "title": "Operating Systems",
  "description": "What an operating system is and how it runs the machine underneath your programs. Chapter 1 takes the grand tour from Operating System Concepts: what an OS does, how a computer boots, interrupts and DMA, the storage hierarchy and caching, multiprocessors and clusters, multiprogramming and timesharing, dual-mode operation and the timer, process, memory, storage and I/O management, protection and security, the data structures inside a kernel, and computing environments from phones to the cloud. Chapter 2 opens up the OS itself: the services it provides, command interpreters and GUIs, system calls and APIs, the six types of system calls with Windows and UNIX examples, system programs and daemons, policy versus mechanism, simple, monolithic, layered, microkernel, modular, and hybrid structures (macOS, iOS, Android), debugging and DTrace, system generation, and booting. Every short reading is followed by a quiz (multiple choice, true or false, and typed answers), with drills, Python exercises (a dual-mode machine, kernel data structures, a shell, a system-call table, structure trade-offs, and a profiler), and a review to close each part.",
